''   Updated.... 14 Sep 2026 -- task 3537 phases 2b3 and 2c (SRC_REV 9, fmt 9): R10-SCAN-RSTALONE, a
''               testResetFault()-alone attempt at each motor's first eligible natural fault (design
''               sec J.1 ruled b) with a BS-RSTALONE record; the -D WD_SELFTEST watchdog self-test
''               (design sec G) with BS-WDTEST-ARM, CP_SELFTEST and the four R11-WDT-* cells; and
''               declareStall() now stops both wheels before it emits anything, in both builds
''   Updated.... 14 Sep 2026 -- task 3540, scan v5 (SRC_REV 10, fmt 10), from run 7 (PL-46,
''               SCAN-RUN-7-EVALUATION.md sec 5): D1 -- refineCliffEdge()/probeConfirmCliff() keep
''               probing 2 degrees closer to the fault, at both speeds, after a clean probe, instead
''               of stopping at the first one; D2 -- half-speed legs now run the rise-on-both-sides
''               negative case for real (bEvaluateHalfBracket()), and hasMinimum() rejects a POOR fit
''               or one bFitPinnedNearLimit() finds pinned against a fault edge, so BS-RESULT-HALF
''               emits no shift/shift_sig from either; D3 -- BS-RESULT's i_min/saving and BS-PAIR2's
''               ratio/imbalance are now judged on each leg's measured floor (legMeasuredFloor()),
''               with the old fitted minimum kept as a new field at the end of each record; D4 --
''               BS-CLIFF gains edge_res_deg_x10, the edge estimate's own +/- resolution; D5 --
''               BS-WALK/BS-BRACKET gain low_net_mV_x10 and BS-LEG gains ref_start_net_mV_x10/
''               ref_end_net_mV_x10, the net figure beside each raw one under an unchanged name; D7
''               -- R9-SCAN-HALFLEG's two per-motor instances carry crit MIN_BRACKETED_NEG/POS
''               instead of one shared CLIFF_PROBED, so they no longer read as duplicates; R9-SCAN-
''               HALFLEG's criterion is now MIN_BRACKETED itself (bEvaluateHalfBracket()'s verdict:
''               FAILs when the leg's low sits at a fault-bounded window edge with nothing measured
''               beyond it toward the fault); main()'s trap value now prints on every run
''               (BS-END trap_value), not only on an abort
''   Updated.... 14 Sep 2026 -- task 3540 coordinator review: probeTowardFault()'s last step before
''               a fault clamps to CLIFF_LAST_STEP_DEG (1 deg, the finest the integer grid allows)
''               instead of refusing to probe at all, so a good/fault gap of exactly
''               CLIFF_HALF_STEP_DEG (2 deg) -- run 7's LEFT POS, good -11 fault -9 -- now gets one
''               probe, at fault -/+ 1 deg, where the plain 2-deg-only version got none
''   Updated.... 16 Sep 2026 -- task 3560 (SRC_REV 12): the panic line no longer claims
''               emergencyCutoff() self-cancels (task 3556 latches it), and the watchdog's gap table
''               names start()'s front-cog handshake and rest-zero capture (tasks 3513, 3556). No record
''               changes shape; the fmt is unchanged
''   Updated.... 18 Sep 2026 -- task 3517 (SRC_REV 13): style-guide conformance, no behaviour change.
''               The six side helpers that chose between wheelR/wheelL calls with ? : now use if/else
''               (PL-29: a ternary ran both calls); bRunScan() and runWatchdogSelfTest() have one exit
''               each; bEvaluateHalfBracket() drops its two unused parameters; DEGREES_PER_TURN is the
''               library's. No record changes shape; the fmt is unchanged
''   Updated.... 18 Sep 2026 -- task 3517 (SRC_REV 14): style guide 5.4.1 -- every method returning one
''               boolean is named is/has/b (bRunScan(), isSideReady(), ...). No record changes
''   Updated.... 19 Sep 2026 -- task 3574 (SRC_REV 17): records are assembled by isp_bench_log (benchLog on
''               cog 0, wdLog on the watchdog cog) instead of this file's two private copies (PL-53) -- the
''               same algorithm, so every record is byte-identical. No record changes.
''
'' =================================================================================================

{ WHY THIS RUN

  Bench Pass 1 (DOCs/analyses/bench/2026-09-12/CHAR-RUN-EVALUATION.md finding 1) measured a
  NEGATIVE motor increment drawing 1.76-1.97x the current of a POSITIVE one, on both motors.
  A negative increment is served by offset_fwd (43 degrees for the 6.5in motor) and a positive
  one by offset_rev (317). DOCs/analyses/BLDC-COMMUTATION-PRINCIPLES.md explains why an offset
  pair symmetric about 0 is right only if the hall pattern's electrical zero sits at 0. This
  binary finds each direction's own minimum-current offset, unattended, from the driver's own
  sense channel -- no meter, no keypress, no operator reading.

  THE APPROACH is util_char_motor.spin2's drive-measure-fault-reset loop, with these deltas:
  independent per-sign offsets (testSetFwdRevIndep), an averaged sense_i_mV over a settled,
  hall-tick-gated window instead of a peak, the fixed bench target as compile-time constants,
  one wheel at a time with the idle wheel never started, no HDMI, and no increment above the
  147_000_000 ceiling logged at 18.5V.

  SEQUENCE
   (0) header records; preflight that each declared wheel turns
   (1) per motor: ZXS_STARTS (5) deliberate restarts, each with a zero (phase XSTART) and a rest,
       wheel floated and nothing commanded; then start checks, zero-current reading, self-check
       at 43/317 (+1/4 and -1/4 speed).
       A miss ends the run: a broken foundation must not produce a sweep that looks like data.
   (2) per motor per sign at 1/4 speed: REF at default, coarse walk out from the default,
       bracket test, interleaved fine points, quadratic fit (one extension), REF again
   (3) per motor per sign at 1/2 speed: re-locate the minimum around the 1/4-speed minimum
   (4) RESULT records: fitted minima, currents, their midpoint and ratio, the 1/2-speed shift

  RUN TIME (scan v3): every point now re-measures its own zero (measureZero() is ZERO_SAMPLES=200
  samples at SAMPLE_MS=5ms, ~1s), so this adds about 1s per point measured, run-wide -- roughly
  1s x (points per leg) x (legs run).

  RUN TIME (fmt 8, task 3537): the XSTART block adds, per motor, ZXS_STARTS x (restart + ready, a
  few ms when the driver reports ready promptly, READY_TIMEOUT_MS 2 s at worst + ~1 s zero +
  REST_MS 0.5 s) -- about 7.6 s per motor nominal, 17.5 s per motor if every ready wait timed out.

  SAFETY, because nobody is watching: an absolute current abort that stops the wheel inside the
  sampling loop, a per-point relative current abort, consecutive and run-wide fault caps, a
  charge cap, a run time cap, both wheels stop() on every exit path, and the QUOTED
  DEBUG_END_SESSION marker. The driver's own angle-lag fault disables PWM regardless.
  PANIC PROCEDURE IS PHYSICAL BATTERY DISCONNECT ONLY.

  RECOVERY after a faulted point sends a ZERO command first, then waits STOPPED: the driver leaves
  DCS_FAULTED only when a changed command arrives. Before commit 67d11f6 (task 3533, PL-28)
  testResetFault() did not send one, so a reset alone waited out its timeout; since that commit it
  sends the zero command itself. The zero-first order is kept because it is the sequence scan runs
  3-5 measured, not because the reset still needs it. ONE exception per motor (fmt 9, design sec
  J.1 ruled b): at that motor's first eligible natural fault, measurePoint() calls testResetFault()
  ALONE, with the faulted non-zero command still set, before the zero command -- R10-SCAN-RSTALONE.
  The unchanged zero-command recovery then follows. No bridge current flows meanwhile: the driver's
  fault branch runs .driveoff before it marks DCS_FAULTED.

  WATCHDOG SELF-TEST (-D WD_SELFTEST, fmt 9, design sec G): the same binary stops after preflight,
  prints BS-WDTEST-ARM, beats CP_SELFTEST once and then stalls on purpose with the wheels at rest.
  The watchdog cog must declare the stall, stop both wheels and emit the four R11-WDT-* verdicts
  itself. If it has not declared after WD_SELFTEST_BACKSTOP_MS, cog 0 resumes and ends the session
  normally with R11-WDT-FIRES FAIL. This build declares only the R11-WDT-* cells and prints bin
  SCANWD.

  SIGN-OFF VERDICTS (task 3537; DOCs/plans/VISIT-SIGNOFF-DESIGN.md sec B, F). Right after BS-BANNER
  come BS-BUILD and one SIGNOFF-DECL per cell this build judges. The scan accumulates per-motor
  facts where it already has them (bStartSide, measureZero, measurePoint, bRunSelfCheck,
  applyOffsets) and emits every declared cell's SIGNOFF instances once, from emitSignoffs(), on
  cog 0's normal end path: after the watchdog is stopped and both wheels are shut down, before
  BS-END. A cell with nothing measured is NOMEAS with n 0, never PASS. On the watchdog path of the
  normal build the only verdict is R11-SCAN-NOSTALL FAIL, emitted by the watchdog cog; the host
  reads every other declared cell with no verdict as NOMEAS. The WD_SELFTEST build's watchdog path
  emits the four R11-WDT-* verdicts instead (see WATCHDOG SELF-TEST above).

  RECORD DISCIPLINE

  Tag-first, comma-separated name,value pairs, all tags prefixed BS-. Every record prints every
  field in the same order; absence is the token NA. Booleans are the tokens TRUE/FALSE. Motors
  are LEFT/RIGHT and increments are signed; this binary never says FWD/REV (PL-19). Every
  numeric field is clamped to a stated width and every token field to a stated length, so no
  record can exceed 280 bytes -- the longest record measured intact through debug(zstr_()).

  Records are assembled by isp_bench_log -- benchLog on cog 0, wdLog on the watchdog cog -- the
  one builder every bench binary shares (PL-53, PL-17), so the digit grouping cannot drift.

  NO timed window contains a debug() statement: every record is emitted after the motor has
  been commanded to zero and has stopped.

  HOW IT IS RUN

      tools/bench-run.sh scan
      tools/bench-run.sh scan-wdtest                        (the -D WD_SELFTEST watchdog self-test)
}

CON { build identity }

    SRC_REV     = 17                                      ' hand-bumped whenever this file changes
    FMT_VERSION = 10                                        ' BS- record-format version

' fmt 10 changes (task 3540, scan v5, PL-46; no existing record's field names, order or content change):
'   - BS-END gains trap_value, printed on every run (not only an abort).
'   - BS-CLIFF gains edge_res_deg_x10.
'   - BS-WALK and BS-BRACKET gain low_net_mV_x10; BS-LEG gains ref_start_net_mV_x10 and
'     ref_end_net_mV_x10.
'   - BS-FIT2/BS-RESULT/BS-RESULT-HALF/BS-PAIR2's min/i_min/i_neg_min/i_pos_min fields are
'     unchanged fitted numbers in BS-FIT2, but BS-RESULT/BS-RESULT-HALF/BS-PAIR2's i_min/i_neg_min/
'     i_pos_min now read each leg's measured floor (D3); the fitted value that used to be there
'     moves to a new field (i_min_fit_mV_x10 / i_neg_min_fit_mV_x10 / i_pos_min_fit_mV_x10) at the
'     end of each record.
'   - hasMinimum() no longer accepts FS_POOR, and rejects a fit bFitPinnedNearLimit() finds pinned
'     against a fault-derived window edge (D2); confirm legs are judged by the real rise-on-
'     both-sides test (bEvaluateConfirmBracket()), not marked bracketed by assignment.
'   - R18-SCAN-LEGBRACKET's criterion is MIN_BRACKETED (D1/D2), and its six per-motor instances each
'     carry a crit naming their sign and rung (D7), replacing the shared CLIFF_PROBED.

' fmt 9 changes (task 3537 phases 2b3 and 2c; no existing record's field names, order or content change):
'   - new BS-RSTALONE record, immediately after BS-POINT3 of the point whose fault took that motor's
'     one testResetFault()-alone attempt: motor, swept_deg, incre, ms, stopped, fault, cleared.
'   - new SIGNOFF-DECL/SIGNOFF cell R10-SCAN-RSTALONE (normal build), one BOOL instance per motor.
'   - new BS-WDTEST-ARM record (WD_SELFTEST build only), just before the deliberate stall.
'   - new checkpoint CP_SELFTEST, token SELFTEST, appended to the CP_* enum.
'   - the WD_SELFTEST build declares only R11-WDT-FIRES/CKPT/STOPPED/STACK and prints bin SCANWD in
'     every sign-off record; the normal build declares none of them.
'   - declareStall() snapshots cog 0's checkpoint and both wheels' BS-WATCHDOG2 values, stops both
'     wheels, and only then emits BS-WATCHDOG/BS-WATCHDOG2 (before fmt 9 it emitted first and stopped
'     after). The records carry the same pre-stop values as before.

' fmt 8 changes (task 3537 phase 2b2; no existing record's field names or order change):
'   - new BS-ZXSALL record per motor, just before BS-END with the SIGNOFF records: the cross-lifetime
'     spread of every driver lifetime's first full zero, per channel, and their count.
'   - BS-BUILD gains zxs_starts and zxs_max_mV_x10 at its end (design sec F.5).
'   - new BS-ZERO phase token XSTART (PH_XSTART, appended to the PH_* enum): the deliberate
'     cross-start zeros before each motor block.
'   - new SIGNOFF-DECL/SIGNOFF cells R8-SCAN-ZXS, R9-SCAN-HALFLEG, R9-SCAN-OWNZERO, R9-SCAN-PAIR2.

' fmt 7 changes (task 3537 phase 2b1; no existing record's field names or order change):
'   - new BS-BUILD record right after BS-BANNER: wd_selftest TRUE/FALSE, sf.
'   - new SIGNOFF-DECL records after BS-BUILD and SIGNOFF records before BS-END (sign-off format
'     sf 1, design sec B.1). The watchdog path emits one SIGNOFF, R11-SCAN-NOSTALL FAIL.
'   - BS-ZERO health_ok prints NA for every phase except ZERO_INIT (PL-34, design sec F.6); before
'     fmt 7 a per-point zero printed the motor block's ZERO_INIT verdict under that label.
'   - a phase or abort-reason value with no token prints UNKNOWN instead of ZERO_INIT / ABORT_I.

' fmt 6 meaning changes (field NAMES and record layouts are unchanged from fmt 5 except the new
' BS-POINT3 record -- these are the same fields now carrying a different, corrected quantity):
'   - BS-FIT2's min_mV_x10/rms_mV_x10 and BS-RESULT's i_min_mV_x10/i_def_mV_x10/saving_pct_x10
'     are net-of-zero (fitLeg() now fits legNetX10, not legMeanX10), not raw.
'   - BS-PAIR2's ratio_raw_x1000 is NA from fmt 6: since the fitted minima it would compare are
'     now net (above), there is no separate "raw" minimum left to ratio, and printing the same
'     number twice under two names would misrepresent it as an independent check. ratio_net_x1000
'     carries the (now the only) ratio, and imbalance is computed from it. See emitPair2().

CON { timing }

    CLK_FREQ = 270_000_000                                  ' every top in this tree runs at 270 MHz
    _clkfreq = CLK_FREQ
    MS_PER_SEC = 1000

' p2kbSpin2Waitms: WAITMS(Milliseconds) must not exceed $8000_0000 clocks, i.e.
' Milliseconds <= $8000_0000 / (CLK_FREQ / MS_PER_SEC). At CLK_FREQ = 270_000_000 that is
' 2_147_483_648 / 270_000 = 7_953 ms (floor). WAITMS_MAX_MS derives this from CLK_FREQ rather
' than hand-copying the number, so a future clock-rate change recomputes it instead of silently
' going stale. Every waitms() argument in this file must be a compile-time-provable value <=
' this ceiling; any "wait a long time" (e.g. an idle-forever loop) must be a repeat of a short
' wait, never one large literal.
    ' +/ (unsigned divide), not / : $8000_0000 is -2_147_483_648 as a signed long, so a signed
    ' divide would give -7_953 (p2kbSpin2Operators: "/" signed division, "+/" unsigned division).
    WAITMS_MAX_MS = $8000_0000 +/ (CLK_FREQ / MS_PER_SEC)   ' 7_953 ms at 270 MHz
    WD_IDLE_MS    = 1_000                                   ' idle-forever chunk, far under the
                                                             ' ceiling above

CON { bench target -- the fixed dual 6.5in Rev B 18.5V rig }

' Hardcoded rather than read from `user`, for the reason test_bench_char.spin2 documents: this
' file needs BOTH a left and a right base, so it names the library's own PINS_*/PWR_*/BRD_*
' constants, which are legal under every config block.
    LEFT_TEST_BASE  = wheelL.PINS_P32_P47                    ' the LEFT board is at P32, measured 2026-09-11
    RIGHT_TEST_BASE = wheelL.PINS_P16_P31
    TEST_VOLTAGE    = wheelL.PWR_18p5V
    TEST_DET_MODE   = wheelL.BRD_AUTO_DET
    MAX_COG_ID      = 7                                      ' start() returns a cog id 0..7 on success

CON { drive increments -- NONE may exceed DRIVE_CEILING_INCRE }

' isp_bldc_motor.spin2 confgurePowerLimits(): 147_000_000 is the 18.5V motor-increment ceiling,
' logged as maxFwdIncreAtPwr at debug_260912-153807.log:471. commandMotor() refuses anything above.
    DRIVE_CEILING_INCRE = 147_000_000
    QTR_SPEED_INCRE     = DRIVE_CEILING_INCRE / 4           ' 36_750_000, quarter speed
    HALF_SPEED_INCRE    = DRIVE_CEILING_INCRE / 2           ' 73_500_000, half speed
    PREFLIGHT_INCRE     = DRIVE_CEILING_INCRE / 8           ' 18_375_000, gentle nudge
    JOG_INCRE           = QTR_SPEED_INCRE                   ' recovery jog, at the self-checked rate

CON { the walking rung -- THE ONE PER-MOTOR BLOCK task 3595 adds (the task-3592 seam) }

' EVERYTHING HERE HAS TO BE RE-DERIVED FOR A DIFFERENT MOTOR, and it is gathered so that the person
' doing it has one place to look rather than a hunt. That is the whole purpose of the block: task 3592
' generalises this instrument to a motor it takes as a parameter, and what had to be edited HERE is
' that task's specification. Record the edits when the Doco motor is first run.
'
' The older per-motor quantities -- DRIVE_CEILING_INCRE and its derived increments above, DEF_NEG_DEG
' and DEF_POS_DEG in the sweep geometry, and the rate and self-check bands below -- are NOT moved into
' this block. They predate it, they are referenced from many places, and relocating them would be
' churn with a transcription risk and no functional gain in a release that is explicitly not doing the
' generalisation. They are named here instead, which serves the same reader.

    ' THE EXPLORATORY WALK'S OWN SPEED, an eighth. The reachable arc is set by the motor and WIDENS as
    '  the commanded speed falls -- MEASURED in run 7 (debug_260914-114703.log): at quarter speed the
    '  torque wall sits at swept +7 down to -11, while at half speed the last clean point on each of
    '  the four legs had already retreated to 8 / -11 / -17 / 9. Every one of those four half-speed
    '  fits came back EDGE or POOR and pinned, because the minimum sat hard against that wall with
    '  nothing measurable below it. Walking slower is what puts room under the minimum.
    '  This increment is not a guess: it is the same value as PREFLIGHT_INCRE, which every run already
    '  uses to nudge both wheels and which is therefore known to turn this motor. It is written out
    '  rather than aliased to PREFLIGHT_INCRE because the two are the same number for different
    '  reasons, and a motor that needed a different walking speed must be able to change one without
    '  silently changing the other.
    LOW_SPEED_INCRE     = DRIVE_CEILING_INCRE / 8           ' 18_375_000, eighth speed -- the walking rung

    ' THE WALKING RUNG'S EXPECTED TICK RATE, by the same law as the other rungs: 98.2 / 2 = 49.1. The
    '  band is the quarter band's own fractional width (-8 % / +8 %) carried down, so no rung is judged
    '  more loosely than another. A rung whose band was widened to make it pass would be measuring its
    '  own tolerance instead of the motor. Per motor because the law runs through ticks per electrical
    '  cycle and the drive-pass rate.
    RATE_LOW_EXPECTED   = 49
    RATE_LOW_LO         = 45
    RATE_LOW_HI         = 53

    ' THE WALKING RUNG'S MEASUREMENT WINDOWS, in hall TICKS. Half the ticks at half the rate is the
    '  same wall-clock second per point, so adding this rung costs points and not longer points -- run
    '  7 used 728 s of a 1_800 s cap. 90 ticks still yields 3 batch means at BATCH_TICKS 30, above
    '  MIN_BATCHES_FOR_SE, so the standard error survives the shorter window. Per motor because a tick
    '  is a hall transition and their count per revolution is the motor's.
    WIN_TICKS_LOW        = 90                               ' 1 wheel revolution at 1/8 speed, fine/confirm points
    WIN_TICKS_COARSE_LOW = 90                               ' and the coarse walk's own points

CON { run sequencing enums }

  ' Which wheel has a running driver cog:
  '  SIDE_NONE  - neither wheel started
  '  SIDE_LEFT  - wheelL started, wheelR never started
  '  SIDE_RIGHT - wheelR started, wheelL never started
    #0, SIDE_NONE, SIDE_LEFT, SIDE_RIGHT
    SIDE_SLOTS = 2

  ' Motor increment sign under test:
  '  SIGN_NEG - negative increments, served by offset_fwd (default 43)
  '  SIGN_POS - positive increments, served by offset_rev (default 317, carried as -43)
    #0, SIGN_NEG, SIGN_POS
    SIGN_SLOTS = 2

  ' Speed rung, slowest first (task 3595):
  '  SPEED_LOW  - LOW_SPEED_INCRE, an eighth. THE WALKING RUNG: the exploratory coarse walk runs here,
  '               because the reachable arc widens as the commanded speed falls.
  '  SPEED_QTR  - QTR_SPEED_INCRE, a confirm rung
  '  SPEED_HALF - HALF_SPEED_INCRE, a confirm rung
  '
  ' SPEED_LOW IS FIRST, WHICH RENUMBERS THE OTHER TWO, and that is safe here where it would not be
  '  elsewhere in this file: a speed reaches the log only through speedToken(), never as an integer, so
  '  no record changes meaning. What it does change is resultSlot()'s arithmetic, which is internal and
  '  derives from SPEED_SLOTS. Slowest-first also lets emitResults() keep walking the rungs in order.
  '
  ' WHICH RUNG WALKS IS A ROLE, NOT A SPEED, and the code asks bWalkRung() rather than naming a speed
  '  -- SPEED_QTR used to carry both meanings at once, which is exactly the shape that drifts.
    #0, SPEED_LOW, SPEED_QTR, SPEED_HALF
    SPEED_SLOTS = 3
  ' WHICH rung walks, named once. Every site that means "the exploratory leg" reads this rather than
  '  naming a speed, so moving the walk to another rung is one edit and not a hunt.
    SPEED_WALK  = SPEED_LOW
    RESULT_SLOTS = SIDE_SLOTS * SIGN_SLOTS * SPEED_SLOTS

  ' Point phase:
  '  PH_SELF      - self-check point at 43/317
  '  PH_REF_START - leg reference at the default offset, before the sweep
  '  PH_COARSE    - coarse walk point
  '  PH_FINE      - fine (or half-speed confirm) point
  '  PH_EXTEND    - one-time extension beyond a fit edge
  '  PH_REF_END   - leg reference at the default offset, after the sweep
  '  PH_CLIFF     - fine-walk probe toward a coarse-walk fault edge (scan v2)
  '  PH_ZERO_INIT - the motor-block's initial zero check, before the self-check (scan v2)
  '  PH_XSTART    - a deliberate cross-start zero, before the motor block (task 3537, fmt 8);
  '                 appended at the end so no existing PH_* value renumbers
    #0, PH_SELF, PH_REF_START, PH_COARSE, PH_FINE, PH_EXTEND, PH_REF_END, PH_CLIFF, PH_ZERO_INIT, PH_XSTART

  ' Point result:
  '  PR_OK             - window completed at speed, no fault
  '  PR_FAULT          - the driver faulted during the point
  '  PR_ABORT_I        - the per-point relative current abort stopped the wheel
  '  PR_STEADY_TIMEOUT - AT_SPEED not reached within STEADY_TIMEOUT_MS
  '  PR_WINDOW_TIMEOUT - the tick-gated window did not complete in time
  '  PR_NO_COG         - the driver cog was not running
    #0, PR_OK, PR_FAULT, PR_ABORT_I, PR_STEADY_TIMEOUT, PR_WINDOW_TIMEOUT, PR_NO_COG

  ' Coarse-walk side stop:
  '  WS_RISE       - current rose RISE_PCT above the low with MIN_PAST points beyond it
  '  WS_FAULTS     - SIDE_FAULT_STOP consecutive faulted points
  '  WS_HARD_LIMIT - reached default +/- HARD_LIMIT_DEG
  '  WS_RUN_ABORT  - the run aborted
  '  WS_DROOP      - task 3595: SIDE_DROOP_STOP consecutive points where the drive stopped FOLLOWING.
  '                  Appended at the end so no existing WS_* value renumbers.
    #0, WS_RISE, WS_FAULTS, WS_HARD_LIMIT, WS_RUN_ABORT, WS_DROOP

  ' Boundary type at one end of the reachable arc (task 3595). The arc is bounded by the MOTOR, not by
  '  the instrument, and the two ends are bounded by DIFFERENT physics -- which is a result, so the
  '  report names it rather than printing a bare pass:
  '  BT_NONE    - this side did not stop on a boundary (still walking, or the run ended)
  '  BT_CURRENT - current-limited: too much lead, the current explodes (ABORT_I)
  '  BT_TORQUE  - torque-limited: too little lead, the motor cannot make the torque (FAULT or DROOP)
  '  BT_RISE    - not a boundary at all: the current rose past its minimum, which is the GOOD stop
  '  BT_LIMIT   - the instrument's own hard limit, reached before the motor's
    ' No BT_COUNT, and none is owed: this file's token helpers are `case` statements with their own
    '  default, not tokenAt() lookups bounded by an enum count. A count here would be a constant with
    '  no consumer, and a reader would go looking for one.
    #0, BT_NONE, BT_CURRENT, BT_TORQUE, BT_RISE, BT_LIMIT

  ' Bracket verdict:
  '  BR_OK              - a high point exists on both sides of the low
  '  BR_EDGE_HARD_LIMIT - a side ran to the hard limit without rising
  '  BR_EDGE_FAULT      - a side ended in faults without rising
  '  BR_NO_VALID        - no valid coarse point at all
  '  BR_RUN_ABORT       - the run aborted during the walk
    #0, BR_OK, BR_EDGE_HARD_LIMIT, BR_EDGE_FAULT, BR_NO_VALID, BR_RUN_ABORT

  ' Fit status:
  '  FS_OK            - convex, interior vertex, residual acceptable
  '  FS_NOT_BRACKETED - no fit: the coarse walk did not bracket a minimum
  '  FS_NOT_CONVEX    - fitted curvature <= 0
  '  FS_TOO_FEW       - fewer valid points than the fit needs, or a degenerate system
  '  FS_EDGE          - vertex within FIT_EDGE_MARGIN_DEG of a span edge after the extension
  '  FS_POOR          - fitted, but the residual exceeds the POOR threshold
  '  FS_SKIPPED       - not attempted
    #0, FS_OK, FS_NOT_BRACKETED, FS_NOT_CONVEX, FS_TOO_FEW, FS_EDGE, FS_POOR, FS_SKIPPED

  ' Leg exit:
  '  LX_DONE      - leg completed
  '  LX_FAULT_CAP - LEG_FAULT_CAP consecutive faulted fine points ended the leg
  '  LX_RUN_ABORT - the run aborted during the leg
  '  LX_SKIPPED   - leg not run (reference invalid, or its prerequisite fit failed)
    #0, LX_DONE, LX_FAULT_CAP, LX_RUN_ABORT, LX_SKIPPED

  ' Abort reason (first cause is kept, never overwritten):
  '  AR_WATCHDOG - appended, task 3534: the watchdog cog declared cog 0 stalled. Appended at the
  '  end, not sorted in with its neighbours, so no existing AR_* value renumbers.
    #0, AR_NONE, AR_PREFLIGHT, AR_START_FAIL, AR_START_CHECK, AR_SELF_CHECK_MISS, AR_ABS_CURRENT, ...
        AR_ABORT_I_CAP, AR_FAULT_CAP, AR_CHARGE_CAP, AR_TIME_CAP, AR_RECOVERY_FAIL, AR_LIB_ABORT, ...
        AR_INCRE_GUARD, AR_OFFSET_READBACK, AR_ABORT_I, AR_WATCHDOG

  ' Abort scope:
  '  SCOPE_RUN   - ends the run
  '  SCOPE_POINT - one point, the run continues
  '  SCOPE_LEG   - one leg, the run continues
    #0, SCOPE_RUN, SCOPE_POINT, SCOPE_LEG

  ' Fault-recovery step:
  '  FX_ZERO_CMD - zero command, wait STOPPED
  '  FX_RESET    - testResetFault()
  '  FX_JOG      - jog at the default offsets
  '  FX_RESTART  - stop() and start() with start checks
    #0, FX_ZERO_CMD, FX_RESET, FX_JOG, FX_RESTART

  ' Self-check first miss:
    #0, MISS_NONE, MISS_START, MISS_ZERO, MISS_I_POS, MISS_I_NEG, MISS_RATIO, MISS_RATE, MISS_INTEG, MISS_FAULT

CON { sampling and windows }

    SAMPLE_MS                = 5                            ' telemetry poll cadence
    SAMPLE_DT_MAX_MS         = 1_000                        ' charge integration step ceiling
    SETTLE_MS                = 1_000                        ' after AT_SPEED, before the window
    STEADY_TIMEOUT_MS        = 4_000                        ' AT_SPEED expected ~0.9 s (1/4) / ~1.3 s (1/2)
    STOP_TIMEOUT_MS          = 3_000                        ' ramp-down ~0.37 s (1/4) / ~0.75 s (1/2)
    READY_TIMEOUT_MS         = 2_000                        ' driver cog reports ready after start()
    REST_MS                  = 500                          ' stopped rest between points
    FAULT_COOL_MS            = 5_000                        ' util_char_motor's post-fault delay
    JOG_MS                   = 1_000                        ' recovery jog duration
    PREFLIGHT_MS             = 700                          ' preflight nudge and coast
    ZERO_SAMPLES             = 200                          ' zero-current reading, ~1 s
    BATCH_TICKS              = 30                           ' 5 electrical cycles per batch mean
    MAX_BATCHES              = 20
    MIN_BATCHES_FOR_SE       = 2
    WIN_TICKS_COARSE         = 180                          ' 2 wheel revolutions
    WIN_TICKS_FINE           = 270                          ' 3 wheel revolutions (also REF at 1/4)
    WIN_TICKS_SELF           = 450                          ' 5 wheel revolutions
    WIN_TICKS_HALF           = 540                          ' 6 wheel revolutions at 1/2 speed
    ' The walking rung's windows live in the per-motor block near the drive increments (task 3595).
    WINDOW_TIMEOUT_MARGIN_MS = 1_000
    ' The shortest drive span worth dividing (task 3595). A point stopped in its first few samples has
    '  no rate to report, and a tick count over a handful of ms is quantisation, not a measurement.
    '  200 ms is about 20 ticks at the quarter-speed rate, well inside the ramp but long enough that
    '  one tick is 5 % rather than all of it.
    SPAN_MIN_MS              = 200
    SAMPLE_CAP               = 8_000                        ' with the clamp below, |sum| <= 5.3e7
    SAMPLE_CLAMP_LO_MV       = -3_300
    SAMPLE_CLAMP_HI_MV       = 6_600
    RATE_X10_SCALE           = 10_000                       ' ticks * this / ms = ticks per second x10
    X10                      = 10                           ' fixed-point scale of every _x10 field
    X100                     = 100                          ' percent / x100 scale
    X1000                    = 1_000                        ' _x1000 scale

CON { expected rates and self-check bands }

    ' DERIVED (PL-50): QTR_SPEED_INCRE x 6 ticks per electrical cycle x 1913.2 drive passes/s / 2^32 = 98.2
    '  ticks/s (one drive pass per 23 ADC frames, isp_bldc_motor.spin2 drvMotor); MEASURED 98.2 at Visit 1.
    '  HALF by the same law: 196.5.
    RATE_QTR_EXPECTED  = 98
    RATE_QTR_LO        = 90
    RATE_QTR_HI        = 106
    RATE_HALF_EXPECTED = 196
    RATE_HALF_LO       = 182
    RATE_HALF_HI       = 212
    ' The walking rung's rates live in the per-motor block above (task 3595).

    ' Pass 1 means /6136: +1/4 90.7/91.6 mV, -1/4 169.5/168.2 mV; bands +/-25%
    POS_QTR_LO_MV      = 68
    POS_QTR_HI_MV      = 114
    NEG_QTR_LO_MV      = 127
    NEG_QTR_HI_MV      = 212
    RATIO_LO_X1000     = 1_550                              ' Pass 1 raw -/+ ratio 1.87 / 1.84
    RATIO_HI_X1000     = 2_200
    ZERO_LO_MV         = -10                                ' Pass 1 fit intercept 9.1 mV (informational only)
    ZERO_HI_MV         = 30

    ' Zero health check: replaces the band-based verdict. Spread from runs 3/4 measured 6-8 mV on both
    ' motors; absolute reads ranged 69-76 mV (right) to 4-12 mV (left). Run 5 showed those absolute
    ' values are re-drawn at every driver start (75 -> 12 mV on the right after a restart): the driver
    ' calibrates its ADCs once from a single settling sample (PL-32), so a zero is a per-start value,
    ' not a board property. The ceiling sits far above the largest per-start zero measured, and far
    ' below a railed ADC (~3300 mV). 300 mV is 2 A equivalent at 150 mV/A: no idle zero can honestly
    ' read that high.
    ZERO_SPREAD_MAX_MV = 10                                 ' max(max - min) of zero samples, mV
    ZERO_ABS_MAX_MV    = 300                                ' max |mean|, mV

    ' Self-check current bands, net of Pass 1's 9.1 mV zero intercept. Derived from raw bands
    ' by subtracting 9 mV: pos_qtr 68-114 -> 59-105; neg_qtr 127-212 -> 118-203.
    ' Run 4 net values (left 83.4/164.0, right 91.6/168.1) fall within these bounds; a dead channel
    ' (frozen zero, net near 0) fails both the zero health check and these bands.
    POS_QTR_NET_LO_MV  = 59                                 ' 68 - 9
    POS_QTR_NET_HI_MV  = 105                                ' 114 - 9
    NEG_QTR_NET_LO_MV  = 118                                ' 127 - 9
    NEG_QTR_NET_HI_MV  = 203                                ' 212 - 9

CON { sweep geometry -- offsets in electrical degrees }

    DEF_NEG_DEG        = 43                                 ' offset_fwd default, negative increments
    DEF_POS_DEG        = 317                                ' offset_rev default, positive increments
    DEGREES_PER_TURN   = wheelL.DEGREES_PER_TURN
    NORM_MAX_STEPS     = 4                                  ' scan offsets stay within +/-360 of range
    COARSE_STEP_DEG    = 10
    HARD_LIMIT_DEG     = 90                                 ' never beyond default +/- this
    COARSE_MAX_STEPS   = HARD_LIMIT_DEG / COARSE_STEP_DEG
    FINE_STEP_DEG      = 5
    CLIFF_HALF_STEP_DEG = 2                                 ' probeTowardFault()'s ordinary step toward a fault edge
    CLIFF_LAST_STEP_DEG = 1                                 ' task 3540 (coordinator review): the finest step the
                                                             ' integer offset grid allows -- probeTowardFault()'s
                                                             ' final probe before a fault, never at or beyond the
                                                             ' point that faulted
    FINE_HALF_DEG      = 20
    CONFIRM_HALF_DEG   = 15
    FINE_ORDER_COUNT   = 9
    CONFIRM_ORDER_COUNT = 7
    EXTEND_PTS         = 4
    CONFIRM_EXTEND_PTS = 2
    RISE_PCT           = 15
    ' Scan v3 (CHANGE 1): the rise threshold for a point inside a fault-bounded window (the last
    ' good point before a coarse-walk fault edge, refined by BS-CLIFF). 4% of run 4's negative-leg
    ' low (20.4 mV) is 0.816 mV; the two points' i_se_mV_x10 was 2 (0.2 mV) each, so their combined
    ' standard error is ~0.283 mV (sqrt(0.2^2+0.2^2)) -- 4% is ~2.9x that combined SE, comfortably
    ' above noise, while still well under the measured rise (8 vs 13: 21.5 vs 20.4mV, +5.4%, ~3.9
    ' combined-SE). RISE_PCT (15%) cannot be met in the <=10 deg between a low and a fault edge.
    FAULT_RISE_PCT     = 4
    MIN_PAST           = 2
    SIDE_FAULT_STOP    = 2
    ' Task 3595, the droop stop. FOLLOW_DROOP_PCT is where "the drive is not following" begins, as a
    '  percent of the commanded rate. It is set OUTSIDE the existing rate band, not inside it: the
    '  band a point must sit in to be VALID data is 90-106 % at quarter speed (RATE_QTR_LO/HI), so a
    '  point between 80 and 90 % is already excluded from the fit while not yet being called a droop.
    '  That gap is deliberate -- the sweep should keep walking through mild raggedness and stop only
    '  when the drive has genuinely given up, and 80 % of commanded is a fifth of the speed missing,
    '  which no healthy point reaches.
    ' NOTE -- ITS NEGATIVE CASE IS THE WHOLE POINT OF THE REBUILD: run 7's 19 non-OK points carry
    '  rate_x10,NA, so NONE of them could have been judged on this at all. A rebuilt run that still
    '  reports NA on a failing point has not fixed anything, and the ARC cell is what says so.
    FOLLOW_DROOP_PCT   = 80
    SIDE_DROOP_STOP    = 2                                  ' consecutive non-following points that end a side
    LEG_FAULT_CAP      = 3
    FIT_MIN_PTS        = 6
    CONFIRM_MIN_PTS    = 5
    FIT_PARAMS         = 3                                  ' quadratic: curvature, slope, level
    MAX_LEG_POINTS     = 48

CON { fit arithmetic (float) }

    FIT_DEG_SCALE       = 10.0                              ' u = (offset - centre) / 10 degrees
    FIT_DET_MIN         = 0.001                             ' below this the normal matrix is degenerate
    FIT_POOR_MIN_MV     = 3.0
    FIT_POOR_SE_FACTOR  = 3.0
    FIT_EDGE_MARGIN_DEG = 1.25                              ' a quarter of a fine step
    FIT_MODEL_CURV      = 0.01523087                        ' (pi/18)^2 / 2: the 1/cos law per (10 deg)^2
    FIT_FLAT_FRACTION   = 0.02                              ' 2% band for flat2_deg_x10
    SHIFT_SIG_FACTOR    = 2.0

CON { safety thresholds }

    ABS_ABORT_MV        = 1_500                             ' 10 A at 150 mV/A; 1.6x the highest Pass 1 sample
    ABS_ABORT_SAMPLES   = 4
    POINT_ABORT_X100    = 250                               ' 2.5x the leg reference
    ' The ceiling that ordering imposes (2026-09-22). The point-scope abort must be able to fire
    '  BEFORE the run-scope absolute abort, or a single high-current point ends the run -- which is
    '  exactly what happened at the half-speed rung, where a 952.9 mV reference put the relative limit
    '  at 2_382 mV against a 1_500 mV absolute. 80 % of the absolute leaves 300 mV of headroom, and
    '  the absolute abort additionally needs ABS_ABORT_SAMPLES consecutive reads, so a point on the
    '  way up trips the survivable guard first with room to spare.
    POINT_ABORT_CEIL_PCT = 80
    POINT_ABORT_CEIL_MV  = (ABS_ABORT_MV * POINT_ABORT_CEIL_PCT) / 100
    POINT_ABORT_SAMPLES = 40
    SPEED_CURRENT_RATIO = 5                                 ' Pass 1 half/quarter current ratio
    RUN_TIME_CAP_S      = 1_800
    RUN_CHARGE_CAP_MVS  = 432_000                           ' 0.8 Ah at 150 mV/A
    RUN_FAULT_CAP       = 24
    RUN_ABORT_I_CAP     = 6
    DRIFT_FLAG_PCT      = 8
    IMBALANCE_PCT       = 10
    UNSETTLED_PCT       = 3

CON { record widths -- every numeric field is clamped to one of these }

    LIM_TINY  = benchLog.LIM_TINY  ' prints at most 4 bytes: -999
    LIM_SMALL = benchLog.LIM_SMALL  ' prints at most 7 bytes: -99_999
    LIM_MID   = benchLog.LIM_MID  ' prints at most 10 bytes: -9_999_999
    LIM_BIG   = benchLog.LIM_BIG  ' prints at most 12 bytes: -999_999_999
    BOOL_BYTES = 5                                          ' FALSE

    ' token field length limits (a longer token prints as "?")
    TOKMAX_MOTOR     = 5
    TOKMAX_SPEED     = 4
    TOKMAX_SIGN      = 3
    TOKMAX_PHASE     = 9
    TOKMAX_RESULT    = 14
    TOKMAX_SERVED    = 10
    TOKMAX_WALK_SIDE = 4
    TOKMAX_WALK_STOP = 10
    TOKMAX_BOUND     = 7                                    ' task 3595: CURRENT is the longest BT_* token
    TOKMAX_BRACKET   = 15
    TOKMAX_FIT       = 13
    TOKMAX_LEG_EXIT  = 9
    TOKMAX_REASON    = 15
    TOKMAX_SCOPE     = 5
    TOKMAX_STEP      = 8
    TOKMAX_VERDICT   = 4
    TOKMAX_MISS      = 5
    TOKMAX_CFG       = 5
    TOKMAX_FRAME     = 5
    TOKMAX_BOARD     = 7
    TOKMAX_ORDER     = 9
    TOKMAX_EXIT      = 8
    TOKMAX_OKFAIL    = 4

CON { sign-off verdicts, task 3537 -- record contract DOCs/plans/VISIT-SIGNOFF-DESIGN.md sec B.1 }

' Sign-off format version, independent of FMT_VERSION. Every limit below is compiled in and printed
' in each SIGNOFF record, so the host catches a binary built with a stale limit.
    SF_VERSION = 1

' Token field length limits (design sec B.3); a longer token prints "?" and cannot match a cell.
' The owning todo-mcp task prints as a plain DAT text token (sSfTask*), never through benchLog.numField(),
' whose digit grouping would print 3_499.
    TOKMAX_TASK      = 5
    TOKMAX_BIN       = 6
    TOKMAX_CELL      = 20
    TOKMAX_CRIT      = 18
    TOKMAX_UNITS     = 8
    TOKMAX_SFVERDICT = 9

' R1-SCAN-COGOK, crit STARTS_COG_BAD, units COUNT. DERIVED (design sec A.4 row 1): the contract is
' start()'s @returns (a cog id 0..7, or -1; isp_bldc_motor.spin2 start()). A start is bad when its
' return is outside 0..7 OR does not equal testGetMotorCog() - 1 (the cog the driver really runs in),
' and the count of bad starts must be 0. MEASURED negative limb: before task 3499 start() returned 0
' while testGetMotorCog() read 2 (DOCs/analyses/bench/2026-09-11/debug_260911-143911.log, T0-10).
    COG_BAD_LO = 0
    COG_BAD_HI = 0

' R2-SCAN-REVB, crit STARTS_NOT_REVB, units COUNT. MEASURED (design sec A.4 row 2): Rev B at all
' eight starts of scan run 5 (SCAN-RUN-5-EVALUATION.md sec 1); the bench boards are both Rev B.
    NOT_REVB_LO = 0
    NOT_REVB_HI = 0

' R3-SCAN-INTEG, crit MISSED_ILLEGAL_SUM, units COUNT. MEASURED (design sec A.4 row 3): missed 0 and
' illegal 0 at every start and every point of scan run 5; DERIVED: at <= 212 ticks/s against the
' driver's 43.9 kHz loop no hall transition can be skipped.
    INTEG_SUM_LO = 0
    INTEG_SUM_HI = 0

' R6-SCAN-OFFSETS, crit READBACK_BAD, units COUNT. MEASURED (design sec A.4 row 6): offsets read back
' exactly at every start of scan run 5; DERIVED: the read-back is integer degrees, so exact.
    READBACK_BAD_LO = 0
    READBACK_BAD_HI = 0

' R7-SCAN-STARTILL, crit STARTS_ILLEGAL, units COUNT. MEASURED (design sec A.4 row 7): illegal 0 at
' every start since the task 3524 fix; DERIVED: before it the first hall read went illegal.
    START_ILLEGAL_LO = 0
    START_ILLEGAL_HI = 0

' R12-SCAN-ALIVE, crit STARTS_NOT_ALIVE, units COUNT. DERIVED from MEASURED (design sec A.5-g): a
' start is alive when its driver reported ready within READY_TIMEOUT_MS AND the first zero of that
' driver lifetime is not frozen (min <> max). A live channel spans 6-8 mV over ZERO_SAMPLES (see
' ZERO_SPREAD_MAX_MV); a status block the driver is not rewriting reads min = max exactly (PL-21).
    NOT_ALIVE_LO = 0
    NOT_ALIVE_HI = 0

' R5-SCAN-SELF (crit NET_I_IN_BAND), R12-SCAN-RATE (crit RATE_IN_BAND) and R11-SCAN-NOSTALL (crit
' ENDED_BY_COG0) are units BOOL: lo TRUE, hi TRUE, printed by emitSignoffBool(). Their bands are the
' scan's own: POS/NEG_QTR_NET_*_MV (MEASURED, design sec A.4 row 5), RATE_QTR_LO..HI (MEASURED, run 5
' self-check 98.1/98.2 ticks/s, row 12), and reaching cog 0's end path (MEASURED: run 5 stopped
' emitting without ending, row 11).
    SIGNOFF_ONE_INSTANCE = 1

' R8-SCAN-ZXS (task 3529), crit ZXS_I/ZXS_U/ZXS_V/ZXS_W, units MV_X10: per motor and channel, the
' spread (max - min) of the ZXS_STARTS deliberate cross-start zero means (design sec A.5-e, sec F.3).
'   DERIVED: a fixed calibration predicts a spread under ~2.5 mV (sigma ~0.47 mV from an 8-frame sum,
'   2.33 sigma range of 5 draws ~1.1 mV, plus ~1 mV lifetime drift; the 8-frame noise reduction is
'   UNVERIFIED, design sec K.1 item 4). Limit = 2 x that prediction = 5.0 mV = 50 mV x10.
'   MEASURED unfixed spreads over three starts (PL-32): LEFT i 14.6, u 0.9, v 13.3, w 2.1 mV;
'   RIGHT i 62.8, u 84.6, v 68.6, w 2.3 mV.
'   Only i and v on both motors, and u on RIGHT, exceeded 5.0 mV unfixed, so only those five
'   instances can FAIL on the unfixed defect. LEFT u and w on both motors are coverage: judged, and a
'   regression above 5.0 mV FAILs, but a PASS there is not evidence of the fix.
'   ZXS_STARTS = 5: five draws make the range statistic 2.33 sigma instead of 1.69 sigma for three
'   (DERIVED, normal range table, design sec A.5-e). Fewer than ZXS_STARTS full zeros -> NOMEAS.
    ZXS_STARTS     = 5
    ZXS_LO_MV_X10  = 0                                      ' a spread is never negative
    ZXS_MAX_MV_X10 = 50                                     ' 5.0 mV, above

  ' Zero channel, index = (sideSlot * ZXS_CHANNELS) + channel:
  '  ZXS_CH_I - sense_i_mV, measureZero()'s meanX10
  '  ZXS_CH_U - sense_u_mV, measureZero()'s senseUmean
  '  ZXS_CH_V - sense_v_mV, measureZero()'s senseVmean
  '  ZXS_CH_W - sense_w_mV, measureZero()'s senseWmean
    #0, ZXS_CH_I, ZXS_CH_U, ZXS_CH_V, ZXS_CH_W
    ZXS_CHANNELS = 4

' R9-SCAN-OWNZERO (task 3530), crit PTS_NO_OWN_ZERO, units COUNT. DERIVED (design sec A.4 row 9):
' scan v4 CHANGE 1/2's invariant is that every valid leg point carries its own lifetime's zero
' (bPtZeroValid), so the count of valid points without one must be 0. COVERAGE, not a falsifier: scan
' v3 already re-measured a zero before every point, so the unfixed code counts 0 too. Run 5's ratio
' 0.440 vs 1.16 came from BS-PAIR2 netting both minima against ZERO_INIT, not from a point without its
' own zero (SCAN-RUN-5-EVALUATION.md sec 6 item 1).
' R18-SCAN-LEGBRACKET (task 3530, criterion redesigned task 3540 PL-46, widened to EVERY leg at task
' 3595 -- it replaces R9-SCAN-HALFLEG, which named the one rung it used to judge), crit
' MIN_BR_<SIGN>_<RUNG> (one instance per sign per rung, six per motor, D7), units BOOL: TRUE when the
' bracket test finds a
' point that rises RISE_PCT (or FAULT_RISE_PCT on a fault-bounded side) above the leg's low on BOTH
' sides, among the points fitLeg() itself fits; NOMEAS when the leg never ran
' (bResLegRan FALSE). FAILs exactly when the low sits at a fault-bounded window edge with nothing
' measured beyond it toward the fault -- the failure mode scan run 7 could not get past even after
' D1's deeper probing resolved three of its four EDGE/POOR fits (SCAN-RUN-7-EVALUATION.md sec 5 item
' 1). The superseded CLIFF_PROBED criterion ("a probe ran") was met by the very defect it existed to
' catch: run 7's four half-speed legs all passed it while none could show a bracketed minimum.
' R9-SCAN-PAIR2 (crit NET_RATIO_ONLY) is units BOOL, lo TRUE hi TRUE. Both BOOL cells print by
' emitSignoffBool().
    OWN_ZERO_MISSING_LO = 0
    OWN_ZERO_MISSING_HI = 0

' R10-SCAN-RSTALONE (task 3533 PL-28 item 1, design sec J.1 ruled b), crit RESET_ALONE_CLEARS, units
' BOOL, one instance per motor, printed by emitSignoffBool(): TRUE when, at that motor's first eligible
' natural fault, testResetFault() ALONE -- the faulted point's non-zero command still set, no zero
' command first -- leaves the wheel stopped and not faulted. The ms it took is in BS-RSTALONE.
'   UNFIXED (DERIVED, isp_bldc_motor.spin2 testResetFault()): before commit 67d11f6 it issued no zero
'   command. An unchanged non-zero command never reaches the driver's .newRqst path, so the reset
'   waited 30 ms + 1000 x 2 ms = 2_030 ms and the wheel stayed FAULTED: stopped FALSE -> measured
'   FALSE -> FAIL.
'   NOMEAS when the motor had no eligible fault (see measurePoint()).

' R11-WDT-* (task 3534, design sec G.4), bin SCANWD: judged only by the -D WD_SELFTEST build, from the
' watchdog cog's declareStall(). FIRES is units MS (WDT_FIRES_LO_MS..WDT_FIRES_HI_MS, watchdog CON
' block below); CKPT, STOPPED and STACK are units BOOL, lo TRUE hi TRUE.
'   FIRES   -- unfixed (no working watchdog): nothing declares, cog 0's backstop prints the elapsed
'              stall, >= WD_SELFTEST_BACKSTOP_MS 12_000, outside 4_000..4_500 -> FAIL.
'   CKPT    -- COVERAGE of the heartbeat contract: a declared checkpoint other than CP_SELFTEST prints
'              FALSE, but no pre-fix binary exists that printed one.
'   STOPPED -- COVERAGE: a driver cog running before the stall and none after the watchdog's stop()s.
'   STACK   -- COVERAGE: WD_STACK_SENTINEL intact at the end of the stall path.

CON { cog-0 progress checkpoints -- watchdog heartbeat contract, task 3534 }

' A checkpoint id names the smallest statement group cog 0 is currently inside, so a stall's
' last checkpoint (reported in BS-WATCHDOG) pins it there and no wider. CP_LOOP_* fire once on
' every pass of the named loop (measurePoint's steady wait, bSettlePoint, bWindowPoint,
' bWaitStopped, measureZero, bDriveForMs, bWaitReady, plus CP_LOOP_REST/CP_LOOP_COOL for the two
' waitms()-only waits -- REST_MS and FAULT_COOL_MS -- that scan run 5's evaluation named as long
' gaps: both are now beat-polled loops instead of one blind waitms(), so they no longer widen
' WD_STALL_MS). CP_RECOVERY_* name bRecoverFromFault()'s four named steps. CP_LIB_* bracket every
' place this file calls into wheelL/wheelR directly (the sideXxx()/commandMotor()/
' shutdownMotors() bodies): the SAME id is set immediately before and immediately after the
' call(s), so a stall parked on a CP_LIB_* id pins it to that one wrapper's body -- it cannot
' distinguish "stuck inside the library call" from "stuck in the return path before the next
' checkpoint", but that residual span is a handful of statements, which is the bracketing this
' task asks for. CP_EMIT brackets lineEmit() itself (every BS- record, every caller, one place).
    #0, CP_INIT, ...
        CP_LOOP_WAITREADY, CP_LOOP_ZERO, CP_LOOP_STEADY, CP_LOOP_SETTLE, CP_LOOP_WINDOW, ...
        CP_LOOP_WAITSTOPPED, CP_LOOP_DRIVEFORMS, CP_LOOP_REST, CP_LOOP_COOL, ...
        CP_RECOVERY_ZERO, CP_RECOVERY_RESET, CP_RECOVERY_JOG, CP_RECOVERY_RESTART, ...
        CP_LIB_COMMAND, CP_LIB_START, CP_LIB_STOP, CP_LIB_COGRUN, CP_LIB_ISREADY, ...
        CP_LIB_ISSTOPPED, CP_LIB_ISFAULT, CP_LIB_RESETFAULT, CP_LIB_DRVSTATE, CP_LIB_TELEMETRY, ...
        CP_LIB_PHASEV, CP_LIB_TICKS, CP_LIB_INTEGRITY, CP_LIB_SETOFFSETS, CP_LIB_GETOFFSETS, ...
        CP_LIB_BOARDTYPE, CP_LIB_SHUTDOWN, CP_EMIT, CP_LOOP_XSTART, CP_LOOP_OWNZERO, CP_SELFTEST
  ' Appended (task 3537 phase 2b2), so no existing CP_* value renumbers:
  '  CP_LOOP_XSTART  - one beat per pass of runMotorBlock()'s deliberate cross-start loop, whose body
  '                    otherwise beats only inside the restart, zero and rest calls it makes
  '  CP_LOOP_OWNZERO - one beat per pass of storeLegOwnZero()'s count over the leg store
  ' Appended (task 3537 phase 2c, design sec F.4):
  '  CP_SELFTEST     - the LAST beat of runWatchdogSelfTest() (WD_SELFTEST build) before its deliberate
  '                    beat-free stall; R11-WDT-CKPT requires the watchdog to report exactly this id

    TOKMAX_CP = 10                                          ' longest checkpoint token, e.g. LIB_ISSTOP

CON { watchdog cog, task 3534 }

' WD_STALL_MS must sit above every legitimate gap this file (or the library methods it calls)
' can leave between two beats. Every loop in this file now beats every SAMPLE_MS (5 ms) pass, and
' REST_MS/FAULT_COOL_MS are beat-polled loops rather than one long waitms() (see the checkpoint
' CON block above), so the only remaining un-instrumented gaps are inside the library methods
' this file calls directly (grep of isp_bldc_motor.spin2 confirms none loop unbounded):
'   - start() -- task 3513's front-cog handshake (<= 100 ms) and task 3556's rest-zero capture
'     (1_000 ms): <= ~1_250 ms.
'   - stop()/testDriveAtMotorIncrement() -- a posted request answered within REQ_ACK_TIMEOUT_MS (20 ms).
'   - every getter -- no waitms().
'   - getBoardType() (sideBoardType, start-of-run only) -- waitms(1) + 500 tight pin reads, low
'     single-digit ms.
'   - testResetFault() (sideResetFault, recovery only) -- worst case 30 ms + 1000 x waitms(2),
'     i.e. up to ~2_030 ms. This is the longest legitimate gap on any path.
' WD_STALL_MS = 4_000 ms leaves ~1_970 ms (about 97%) of margin over that ~2_030 ms worst case,
' and is 16x WD_POLL_MS, so ordinary poll jitter cannot cross it by accident.
    WD_POLL_MS    = 250                                     ' watchdog sample cadence
    WD_STALL_MS   = 4_000                                   ' declare a stall above this many ms

' R11-WDT-FIRES band (design sec G.4), DERIVED from watchdog(): the reported msSinceBeat is >= WD_STALL_MS
' by construction, and at most one poll of sampling offset plus one poll of scheduling jitter later.
    WDT_FIRES_LO_MS = WD_STALL_MS                           ' 4_000
    WDT_FIRES_HI_MS = WD_STALL_MS + (2 * WD_POLL_MS)        ' 4_500

' WD_SELFTEST's backstop (design sec G.3), DERIVED: 3 x WD_STALL_MS = 12_000 ms, 2.7x the latest expected
' declaration (WDT_FIRES_HI_MS 4_500), so a working watchdog is never pre-empted, while a dead one cannot
' hang the session -- cog 0 resumes, returns to main() and ends it normally.
    WD_SELFTEST_BACKSTOP_MS = 3 * WD_STALL_MS               ' 12_000

' Stack sizing per p2kbAppNoteP2an006SizingCogTaskStacks: the deepest path only runs at a stall, which
' cannot be exercised off the bench, so the app note's "start at 128, measure, then shrink to ~1.5x"
' cycle cannot be closed here. Counting frames by its rule (params + returns + locals, packed in
' declaration order, summed over every frame simultaneously on the stack at the deepest point) instead
' of measuring. Task 3537 phase 2c re-count (fmt 9: declareStall() gained two locals and calls
' wdSnapshotStall(); emitWatchdog() reads that snapshot instead of six locals; the WD_SELFTEST build
' adds wdEmitSignoff()):
'   watchdog()            0 params + 0 returns + 3 locals                       =  3
'   declareStall()         1 param  + 0 returns + 2 locals                       =  3
' deepest, WD_SELFTEST build -- R11-WDT-FIRES's measured field:
'   wdEmitSignoff()       10 params + 0 returns + 2 locals                       = 12
'   wdLog.naNumField()     4 params + 0 returns + 0 locals                       =  4
'   (isp_bench_log's own, PL-53 -- the same frames the retired wd* copies had:)
'   numField()             3 params + 0 returns + 0 locals                       =  3
'   lineField()            2 params + 0 returns + 0 locals                       =  2
'   lineAddNum()           1 param  + 0 returns + 14 locals (digitStack[11] + 3) = 15
'   lineAddChar()          1 param  + 0 returns + 0 locals                       =  1
'                                                                         total  = 43 longs
' other chains, for comparison:
'   normal build, wdEmitSignoffBool() (7 + 1 = 8) n field:  3 + 3 + 8 + 3 + 2 + 15 + 1 = 35 longs
'   emitWatchdog() (1 + 0 = 1) numeric field:               3 + 3 + 1 + 3 + 2 + 15 + 1 = 28 longs
'   wdEmitSignoff() token field -> wdLog.tokenField() 3 -> bTokenFits() (2 + 1 + 1 = 4): 3 + 3 + 12 + 3 + 4 = 25
'   wdSnapshotStall() (0) -> isp_bldc_motor testGetTelemetry() (0 + 8 returns + 0 = 8):  3 + 3 + 0 + 8 = 14
'   wheelL.stop() (0) -> bHoldsPinBaseClaim() (0 + 1 return + 0 = 1):                  3 + 3 + 0 + 1 = 7
' -- comfortably under half of 128, so WD_STACK_LONGS = 128 (the app note's own "can't overflow
' while measuring" starting point) is used directly rather than shrunk to ~1.5x an unmeasured
' number. The frame count does not add the interpreter's own per-call bookkeeping, which the app
' note's measured-and-shrunk cycle would normally absorb; WD_STACK_SENTINEL is the runtime check
' that stands in for that missing measurement (declareStall() reports it in BS-WATCHDOG2).
    WD_STACK_LONGS = 128
    WD_STACK_SENTINEL = $5A5A_5A5A                          ' filled before cogspin, checked at
                                                             ' the end of the stall path

OBJ { objects under test }

#ifdef BENCH_CFG
    user   : "isp_bldc_motor_userconfig_bench"              ' banner cfg_id and motor_type only
#else
    user   : "isp_bldc_motor_userconfig"
#endif
    wheelL : "isp_bldc_motor"                                ' left wheel instance
    wheelR : "isp_bldc_motor"                                ' right wheel instance
    benchLog : "isp_bench_log"                              ' the record builder (PL-53) and the cog-event quiet window (PL-41, PL-85)
    wdLog    : "isp_bench_log"                              ' the watchdog cog's own record builder: cog 0 may be stalled mid-record in benchLog

